WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.030
Another day, another tidy explanation of what
seems to be a gaping plot hole in Avengers:

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:05.520
Endgame.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.260
As ever, major spoilers ahead.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.880
The flick's directors, Joe and Anthony Russo,
addressed a bunch of burning questions during

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.500
a recent Q&amp;A in China.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.120
Among these: why couldn't Doctor Strange simply,
say, portal Thanos' Infinity Gauntlet hand

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.240
into another dimension?

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.780
Now, this is an excellent question for one
simple reason: those yellow sparklies have

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.780
proven to be quite effective as a weapon.

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:29.910
In Avengers: Infinity War, during Wong's battle
with Cull Obsidian, the gigantic beast had

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.050
his arm severed by one of the portals.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:35.280
Why couldn't Doctor Strange have done the
exact same thing to Thanos during Endgame's

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.280
climactic clash?

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.380
Here's the Russos' response, according to
We Got This Covered:

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.940
"Thanos' skin is almost impenetrable, we don't
know whether Doctor Strange had the capability

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:44.440
to do it.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.280
If he failed to cut it on time, Thanos would
still be able to do the snap.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.840
Doctor Strange realized this issue during
his millions of test runs."

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.060
That last part actually seems like an extremely
strong explanation.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.120
You could rely on that as an answer to just
about any variation of the same question,

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.480
and it kind of defies second-guessing.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.990
Strange didn't attempt to lay the old magical
arm-chopping trick on Thanos because he knew

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:06.580
it wouldn't work.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.840
In fact, he had already seen every possible
strategy for defeating the Mad Titan, 14,000,605

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.100
of them, to be exact, during his future viewing-fest
on Titan in Infinity War.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.609
And he alone was privy to the one strategy
out of all of those that would work.

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:23.840
In order for Thanos to ultimately fail, he
would first have to succeed.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.780
Strange would have known that there was no
way to stop him from gathering all of the

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.340
Infinity Stones, and no way to prevent the
Decimation from happening.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.920
Hence his one condition for voluntarily offering
up the Time Stone to Thanos: that Tony Stark's

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:35.700
life be spared.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.240
Without Stark around to perfect the technology
for time travel, the Avengers' strategy of

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.720
deploying teams to various points in time
to pre-emptively acquire all of the stones

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.280
wouldn't have been possible.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.720
Strange knew, as well, that only Stark, who
designed the Infinity Gauntlet 2.0, would

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.060
have the wherewithal to pull the ol' Gauntlet
switcheroo on Thanos at the crucial moment.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:58.069
That's the beauty of the Russos' explanation:
it's basically bulletproof, and it applies

00:01:58.069 --> 00:02:00.380
to literally any alternate scenario.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.450
Why didn't our heroes attempt the same trick
they had on Titan, using Mantis to distract

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.700
Thanos until someone could yank his Gauntlet
off?

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.260
Well, because Strange knew it wouldn't work.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.019
Why didn't Stark use his nearly all-powerful
nanotech to build a Thanos-sized suit of armor

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.220
that would trap the Mad Titan and fly him
into the sun?

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.460
Because Strange knew it wouldn't work.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.180
Why didn't Captain Marvel just crater his
big purple butt?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.700
Ask Doctor Strange.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.440
He'll tell you it's because he knew it wouldn't
work.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.319
The more you think about Strange's viewing
of those millions of possible outcomes, the

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:31.650
more mind-blowing it becomes as a storytelling
device, or a convenient little storytelling

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.540
cheat, depending on your point of view.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.560
On the surface, the line seems to illustrate
for the audience just how stacked the odds

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.500
were against our heroes.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.240
But more than that, it also allows Marvel
to dismiss any and all questions beginning

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.820
with "Why couldn't they have just" in one
fell swoop.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.299
At any rate, the Russos have been busy shutting
down all the "full of plot holes" talk since

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:54.360
Endgame's release, and their explanations
should only motivate you to take in repeat

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.530
viewings at your earliest convenience to see
if they check out.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.700
Armed with their insider knowledge, you'll
probably want to go check it out another time

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.540
or two while it's still in theaters.

